1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:08,696
Five points, joined by springs. We want to cut them into two teams, and keep the stiff springs inside.

2
00:00:08,696 --> 00:00:17,596
Sounds like a graph problem. It is. But the trick is surprising: we will turn it into a line first.

3
00:00:17,596 --> 00:00:19,420
Let's go.

4
00:00:20,270 --> 00:00:25,906
The number on a spring is its stiffness: five is stiff, one is soft.

5
00:00:25,906 --> 00:00:34,206
A, B and C are tied by springs of five, four and one. D and E by a spring of five.

6
00:00:34,206 --> 00:00:39,486
Between the two groups there are only two soft springs: two, and one.

7
00:00:40,336 --> 00:00:48,636
Two rules. Every spring we cut, we pay its stiffness. And neither team can be tiny.

8
00:00:48,636 --> 00:00:59,124
Why the second rule? Because the cheapest cut would isolate one point. E alone costs six. That is not a team.

9
00:00:59,974 --> 00:01:04,578
You might think: just try every split, keep the cheapest.

10
00:01:04,578 --> 00:01:09,950
For five points there are sixteen splits. Fine.

11
00:01:09,950 --> 00:01:16,090
For a thousand points the number has hundreds of digits. Not fine.

12
00:01:16,090 --> 00:01:18,562
So we need a shortcut.

13
00:01:19,412 --> 00:01:25,000
The shortcut: give every point a score. One number each.

14
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:31,356
Stiff springs should keep their ends close. Soft springs can stretch.

15
00:01:31,356 --> 00:01:36,324
We do not pick the scores. The springs decide them.

16
00:01:37,174 --> 00:01:43,482
Treat the scores as positions on a line. Let go, and the points settle.

17
00:01:43,482 --> 00:01:48,662
But fix the length of the line, or they all collapse into one spot.

18
00:01:48,662 --> 00:01:57,590
A, B and C pull left. D and E pull right. The soft spring between them becomes a wide gap.

19
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:02,900
That list of scores has a name: the Fiedler vector.

20
00:02:02,900 --> 00:02:09,664
It is not a point, not an edge, not a cut. It is one number per point.

21
00:02:09,664 --> 00:02:14,752
Its job: tell each point how far left or right it wants to sit.

22
00:02:15,602 --> 00:02:21,958
Now sorting. Take the five numbers and line them up, smallest first.

23
00:02:21,958 --> 00:02:26,730
Negative values go left, positive values go right.

24
00:02:26,730 --> 00:02:32,466
If two values tie, break the tie by name, so the result never wobbles.

25
00:02:33,316 --> 00:02:41,184
Why bother sorting? Because on a sorted line, a cut is just a prefix: the first k points.

26
00:02:41,184 --> 00:02:48,908
Four gaps, so four candidates. Without sorting, you are choosing among sixteen subsets.

27
00:02:48,908 --> 00:02:52,772
Sorting turns 'which side' into 'how many on the left'.

28
00:02:53,622 --> 00:02:57,050
So what do these two actually do here?

29
00:02:57,050 --> 00:03:05,182
The eigenvector is the calmest seating. It becomes the scores we sort, and the scores are what we cut between.

30
00:03:05,182 --> 00:03:13,626
The eigenvalue is the price of that seating. Small means the graph splits easily; large means it does not.

31
00:03:13,626 --> 00:03:19,434
Neither is a step you perform. They are what the algorithm hands back.

32
00:03:20,284 --> 00:03:23,592
So here is the ledger for our graph.

33
00:03:23,592 --> 00:03:31,700
Diagonal: the total stiffness at each point. Off-diagonal: minus the stiffness of that spring.

34
00:03:31,700 --> 00:03:36,260
Every row sums to zero. That is your check.

35
00:03:37,110 --> 00:03:42,098
The ledger gives one number for any arrangement: total stretch.

36
00:03:42,098 --> 00:03:48,070
Each spring contributes its stiffness, times the squared gap between its ends.

37
00:03:48,070 --> 00:03:55,222
Stiff and far apart is expensive. So the calmest arrangement keeps stiff springs short.

38
00:03:56,072 --> 00:04:01,492
Try a real split: plus one on one side, minus one on the other.

39
00:04:01,492 --> 00:04:08,832
A cut spring has gap two, so it costs four. An uncut spring costs zero.

40
00:04:08,832 --> 00:04:12,764
So total stretch equals four times the cut.

41
00:04:12,764 --> 00:04:19,484
Good split: three, so twelve. Bad seed: twelve, so forty-eight.

42
00:04:20,334 --> 00:04:24,362
So find the arrangement with the smallest stretch.

43
00:04:24,362 --> 00:04:34,078
Two catches. All-equal gives zero, but that is not a split. And with a free length, everything shrinks to zero.

44
00:04:34,078 --> 00:04:41,590
Fix the length, ban the flat one, minimise. The answer is the second-smallest eigenvector.

45
00:04:42,440 --> 00:04:48,892
Now sweep. Four gaps: six, three, five, six.

46
00:04:48,892 --> 00:04:51,556
Keep the cheapest: two.

47
00:04:52,406 --> 00:04:56,890
The gap nearest the middle costs five, not three.

48
00:04:56,890 --> 00:05:00,678
Counting points knows nothing about stiffness.

49
00:05:00,678 --> 00:05:06,606
So we check every gap. The scores propose; the cut decides.

50
00:05:07,456 --> 00:05:15,180
The answer: A, B, C on one side, D and E on the other. Cost three.

51
00:05:15,180 --> 00:05:18,612
Both stiff springs stay inside a team.

52
00:05:19,462 --> 00:05:22,890
Let's put the whole chain in one picture.

53
00:05:22,890 --> 00:05:27,422
Graph, scores, sorted line, cut, check.

54
00:05:27,422 --> 00:05:31,382
If you can redraw this from memory, you have got it.

55
00:05:32,232 --> 00:05:37,940
Three traps. The scores can flip sign; same teams.

56
00:05:37,940 --> 00:05:42,904
A disconnected graph breaks the method, so check that first.

57
00:05:42,904 --> 00:05:46,020
And ties need a stable sort.

58
00:05:46,020 --> 00:05:51,564
Now try the exercises. They only use what we just did.

